  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{{t}^{3}}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=24.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =-\\frac{3}{(1)^{2}}+C \\\\ 24 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+27.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}+27 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{{t}^{2}} & =27 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, we discard solution $t=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$",
